{"resourceIdentifier":"3280","resourceName":"Carpathia Forest Carbon Project","description":"The Carpathia Forest Carbon Project aims for the restoration and full protection of forests in the south-eastern Carpathians Mountains in Romania. Through land purchase and conservation agreements, the project seeks to protect primary and secondary forests in five Natura 2000 network sites and regenerate areas affected by clear cuts, spruce monocultures, and degraded riparian habitats along the mountain rivers.\r\nThe Carpathia Forest Carbon Project would create GHG emission reductions by avoiding the release of carbon associated with timber harvesting, road building and other forestry operations that would be expected to be carried out in the baseline (business-as-usual) scenario. Thus, the project includes improved forest management (IFM) practices that convert the logged forests to protected forests through conservation management activities with low level of timber removal for ecosystem/habitat enhancement and risk management in the wider Făgăraș Mountains (Natura 2000 designated site), the Piatra Craiului National Park and the Leaota Mountains. \r\nThe broader vision of this project is to establish the foundations of a new National Park that can be recognized as the European Yellowstone Park (in reference to the Yellowstone National Park in the United States), encompassing the Carpathian Mountains; from the Olt Valley in the West to Piatra Craiului Mountain in the East.\r\nThe project area involves the lands owned by Fundatia Conservation Carpathia (FCC) as well as those managed by them and owned by their partners Wildland Ltd, Romfor Sustainable Forestry SRL, Almimax Natura, Dante International and Sanatate & Natura. The forest properties to be considered include 36 forest management plans (FMP). The total project area is 23,404 ha, of which 14,541 ha are eligible for the development of the IFM activities.","location":{"latitude":45.376212,"longitude":25.026965},"inPub